## 1. Purpose

This document establishes the Preventive Maintenance (PM) program framework for MMC {{ plant_name }}. The program ensures equipment reliability, maximizes uptime, reduces unplanned downtime, and maintains safe operating conditions for all production and support equipment.

---

## 2. Scope

This program applies to:
- All production equipment on Lines 1, 2, and 3
- All support and auxiliary equipment
- All facilities equipment (HVAC, electrical distribution, compressed air, etc.)
- All material handling equipment
- All safety-critical systems (guarding, interlocks, emergency systems)

**Operating Context:**
- 3-shift operation (24/5) with weekend maintenance windows
- High-mix / medium-volume manufacturing environment
- Maintenance performed by in-house technicians and authorized contractors

---

## 3. Maintenance Philosophy

MMC {{ plant_name }} employs a balanced maintenance strategy:

| Strategy | Application | Percentage of Program |
|----------|-------------|----------------------|
| **Preventive Maintenance (PM)** | Fixed-schedule tasks based on time or cycles | 60% |
| **Condition-Based Maintenance (CBM)** | Monitoring-driven tasks on critical assets | 20% |
| **Predictive Maintenance (PdM)** | Vibration, thermal, oil analysis on key equipment | 10% |
| **Reactive Maintenance** | Unplanned repairs (target: minimize) | 10% (target <15%) |

### 3.1 Maintenance Priorities

| Priority | Definition | Response Time | Examples |
|----------|------------|---------------|----------|
| **Emergency (P1)** | Safety hazard or complete production stop | Immediate | Press failure, robot fault, conveyor down |
| **Urgent (P2)** | Degraded operation or imminent failure | Within 4 hours | Partial system failure, quality impact |
| **Scheduled (P3)** | Planned PM or non-critical repair | Per schedule | Routine PM, planned repairs |
| **Deferrable (P4)** | Low impact, can wait for opportunity | Next available window | Cosmetic, minor adjustments |

